<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ==============================================================================================
  AWS CardDemo - Maven module descriptor for the COBOL to Java 21 / Spring Boot 3.x migration.

  This is the ONLY build manifest in the repository. Before this module existed there was no
  automated build at all: README.md step 4 ("Compile the Programs") directs the reader to "the
  compile process followed by your mainframe shopfloor". The entire Maven build, the application
  entry class and the application configuration are therefore net-new scaffolding, created from
  nothing (AAP implicit requirement I7). There is also no aggregator pom at any level of this
  repository, which is why the <parent> below carries an EMPTY <relativePath/> - see the note
  beside it; omitting that single element is the most common way this file breaks.

  WHAT THIS MODULE IS
    A like-for-like translation of the 28 COBOL programs in app/cbl into Java - not a redesign.
    The 17 CICS online programs become stateless REST controllers, 9 non-CICS programs become
    Spring Batch jobs, and the 2 CALLed subprograms become injected Spring components. Every
    observable output - record bytes, field values, numeric scale, message text and return codes -
    is held identical to what the COBOL produces today. 17 + 9 + 2 = 28, with no program counted
    twice and none left out.

  THE JOB COUNT IS NINE, DERIVED FROM SOURCE - AND THE AAP PROSE SAYS TEN
    This is worth setting out precisely, because the discrepancy is real and a reader who finds
    only one of the two numbers will assume a job is missing.

    Counting EXEC PGM= across app/jcl and app/proc yields exactly 8 distinct CardDemo programs:
    CBACT01C (READACCT.jcl), CBACT02C (READCARD.jcl), CBACT03C (READXREF.jcl), CBACT04C
    (INTCALC.jcl), CBCUS01C (READCUST.jcl), CBSTM03A (CREASTMT.JCL), CBTRN02C (POSTTRAN.jcl) and
    CBTRN03C (TRANREPT.jcl, and again in TRANREPT.prc - two sites, one program). Add CBTRN01C,
    which no JCL anywhere invokes and which migrates as a fully runnable job with no trigger
    (AAP implicit requirement I4, gate G13), and the total is NINE. BatchConfig.JobContracts
    enforces exactly those nine keys at startup, so the number is checked rather than asserted.

    AAP prose states ten in its summary line, and the cause is identifiable: CBCUS01C is counted
    once among the eight EXEC PGM= programs and a second time as a separately named customer file
    reader. The AAP's own detailed job table lists nine rows, and its dependency note reads "the 8
    JCL-invoked programs plus the orphan CBTRN01C", so nine is consistent with the AAP everywhere
    except that one summary. The conflict is recorded here rather than resolved by inventing a
    tenth job, which is what a manifest claiming ten would eventually invite (practice B4).

    NEITHER SUBPROGRAM IS A JOB, despite what their prompt-mandated class names suggest.
    CBSTM03B is CBSTM03A's data-access collaborator, called at 13 sites, and becomes a @Component;
    CSUTLDTC is a called date-validation subprogram, invoked from two ONLINE programs, and becomes
    a @Service. Neither has an EXEC PGM= anywhere in app/jcl or app/proc (AAP I3, gate G12). The
    names StatementGenerationJobB and DateUtilityJob end in "Job" because the build prompt fixed
    them; the behaviour comes from the source, not from the name (AAP rule R1).

  VERSION POLICY (AAP 0.5.2 - 0.5.4; binding practices B1 and B2)
    * Java 21 (LTS)  -> <release>21</release>, and nothing newer. Verified on JDK 21.0.11.
    * Apache Maven   -> 3.9 or newer is required; this build is verified with 3.9.16. The
                        distribution's 3.8.7 candidate sits below that floor and was rejected.
                        No enforcer plugin polices the floor, deliberately: enforcer is outside
                        the closed plugin set, and adding it would be scope creep (practice B4).
    * spring-boot-starter-parent 3.5.16 -> the highest published 3.x release. The 4.x parent
                        line, and the Spring Batch 6.x line that belongs to it, were both
                        evaluated and DELIBERATELY REJECTED: the build prompt pins Spring Boot
                        3.x, and constraint fidelity outranks recency (practice B2). 3.5.16 is
                        a deliberate pin, not staleness - please do not "helpfully" upgrade it.
    * Spring Batch 5.2.6, JUnit 5, Mockito, AssertJ, HikariCP, Jackson, Jakarta Validation, the
                        embedded servlet container and H2 all take their versions from the
                        parent BOM, so NONE of them names a <version> here and NONE of the BOM's
                        own version properties is redeclared here either. Only the three build
                        plugins named in AAP 0.5.4 carry an explicit <version>. Every version in
                        this file is a fixed, published release: there is no placeholder and no
                        moving version marker anywhere in it (gate G2, practice B1).

  THE DEPENDENCY INVENTORY IS FROZEN, INCLUDING THE BOM'S OWN PROPERTIES
    AAP 0.5.4 fixes the resolved inventory this module is accepted against, and two of its entries
    are transitive rather than declared: tomcat-embed-core 10.1.55 and the Jackson BOM 2.21.4,
    both managed by spring-boot-dependencies-3.5.16. Redeclaring the BOM properties tomcat.version
    or jackson-bom.version here would move those two families off the frozen inventory just as
    surely as naming a <version> on a dependency, because a BOM property is version MANAGEMENT and
    the resolved graph is what the plan pins. Neither property is redeclared, and neither may be:
    the frozen inventory is changed at the plan, not in this file (practices B2 and B4).

    A KNOWN RESIDUAL RISK, RECORDED RATHER THAN SILENTLY ABSORBED (practice B12). 3.5.16 is the
    last published OSS 3.5.x release, so no upgrade exists inside the pinned line, and both managed
    families sit in advisory-affected ranges: Apache lists 10.1.55 among the versions fixed by
    10.1.56 and 10.1.57, and jackson-databind 2.21.4 falls in the range fixed by 2.21.5. Direct
    exploitability is not established for this application - the implicated optional Tomcat
    features are not enabled and Jackson case-insensitive property matching is not switched on -
    and the module declares no dependency of its own on either family. Closing the advisory gap
    nonetheless needs one of two things that only a plan-level decision can supply: an authorized
    update to AAP 0.5.4's inventory, or a commercially supported patched 3.x BOM (unavailable to
    this build, which resolves solely from repo1.maven.org with no credentials and no private
    registry). Until then the resolved versions are the BOM's, as the plan states them.

  CLOSED DEPENDENCY SET (AAP 0.5.6)
    Exactly six dependencies are declared below and nothing may be added to them. No technology
    enumerated in AAP 0.5.6 is DECLARED here: no object-relational mapper, no schema-migration
    tool, no relational database driver, no authentication or credential-hashing library, no cloud
    service SDK, no metrics registry or tracing exporter, no container-based test harness, no
    reactive web stack, no API-documentation generator, no compile-time accessor or mapper
    generator, and no third-party COBOL copybook parser. The fixed-width record codecs are
    hand-written precisely so that every byte offset stays reviewable against its copybook
    (practice B11), and the sign-on credential comparison is preserved exactly as the COBOL
    performs it, which is why no authentication library appears here (practice B6).

    Note the wording: not DECLARED. For one prohibited family the distinction matters, and
    claiming outright absence would be untrue - see the next section.

  OBSERVABILITY: WHAT THE PROHIBITION REACHES, AND WHAT IT CANNOT
    AAP 0.5.6 excludes Micrometer, Prometheus, Grafana and Jaeger. Nothing of the sort is declared
    below, no registry implementation is on the classpath, no exporter, no Actuator, no tracing
    bridge, no management endpoint and no observability configuration exists anywhere in this
    module - so nothing is ever collected, aggregated, exported or scraped.

    But five artifacts DO appear in the effective graph, and it is honest to name them, with their
    resolved scopes read off dependency:tree rather than assumed:
        io.micrometer:micrometer-observation:1.15.12   compile  <- org.springframework:spring-web
        io.micrometer:micrometer-commons:1.15.12       compile  <- micrometer-observation
        io.micrometer:micrometer-core:1.15.12          compile  <- spring-batch-core
        org.hdrhistogram:HdrHistogram:2.2.2            runtime  <- micrometer-core
        org.latencyutils:LatencyUtils:2.0.3            runtime  <- micrometer-core
    Both entry points are non-optional transitives of starters the AAP itself MANDATES.

    They were not left in place for convenience. Removing them was attempted and measured, and
    it is not binary-compatible. jdeps over spring-batch-core-5.2.6.jar and spring-web-6.2.19.jar
    shows references to io.micrometer.core.instrument and io.micrometer.observation, and javap
    locates them in AbstractJob and AbstractStep - the base classes of EVERY Spring Batch job and
    step, so they are always loaded, not loaded on a feature path. A link probe against the real
    test classpath with the five artifacts removed produced:
        NoClassDefFoundError io/micrometer/core/instrument/MeterRegistry
            <- org.springframework.batch.core.job.AbstractJob
            <- org.springframework.batch.core.step.AbstractStep
        NoClassDefFoundError io/micrometer/observation/ObservationConvention
            <- org.springframework.web.filter.ServerHttpObservationFilter
            <- org.springframework.web.client.RestTemplate
    With the artifacts present, all four classes link. An <exclusions> block would therefore not
    slim the build; it would stop every batch job and the web layer from loading at all.

    THIS IS AN INTERNAL CONFLICT IN THE AAP, AND IT IS ESCALATED RATHER THAN PAPERED OVER
    (practice B12). The AAP mandates spring-boot-starter-web and spring-boot-starter-batch and
    simultaneously excludes Micrometer; on Spring Framework 6 and Spring Batch 5 those two
    requirements cannot both hold, because the observation API is part of how those frameworks are
    built. Only a human can decide which requirement yields. What this file will not do is claim
    the exclusion is satisfied while the artifacts sit in the graph, or delete them and ship a
    build that cannot start. The spirit of the prohibition - no metrics collected, no telemetry
    emitted, no observability stack operated - is fully honoured; its letter, as applied to
    transitive API jars of mandated starters, is not achievable.

  PERSISTENCE AND THE INTENTIONALLY ABSENT DRIVER (AAP 0.5.5; residual risk R-E)
    Data access is plain JDBC over the existing VSAM and sequential datasets: no DDL, no schema
    change, no new database, no generated table definitions. There is zero EXEC SQL in all 28
    programs, README.md lists relational and hierarchical database support as Roadmap items
    rather than current behaviour, and indexed VSAM has no standard published JDBC driver -
    so no driver coordinate is compiled into this build. The DataSource is bound entirely from
    src/main/resources/application.yml, keyed by the dataset names defined in
    app/csd/CARDDEMO.CSD and the batch-only DD names in app/jcl, and the site-specific mainframe
    driver is a DEPLOYMENT-TIME input. Residual risk R-E is accepted and recorded here:
    production connectivity cannot be exercised in this environment, so the repositories are
    validated against the fixture-backed parity harness instead, with H2 confined to TEST scope.

  THE GATE
    One command performs the whole cycle, non-interactively and with no watch mode:
        mvn -f app/java/pom.xml clean verify
    It compiles under Java 21, runs every unit test and all 560 parity cases with results
    streamed to stdout, and then enforces at least 90% BRANCH coverage - at bundle level AND
    independently per package, so that no package can hide behind another package's coverage
    (gate G49). BRANCH rather than LINE is the counter that matters here: the migration is judged
    on 508 COBOL 88-level condition names, 111 EVALUATE statements and the guard chains that
    surround them.

  This build reads and writes only inside app/java. The reference trees app/cbl, app/cpy,
  app/cpy-bms, app/bms, app/jcl, app/proc, app/csd, app/ctl, app/catlg and app/data are the sole
  oracle for behavioural equivalence, and no plugin declared here reads from, generates into or
  rewrites any of them (practice B3, gate G5).
  ==============================================================================================
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!-- The empty <relativePath/> is MANDATORY and must not be removed. This repository contains
         no aggregator pom at any level (app/pom.xml does not exist and neither does one at the
         root), so app/java is a standalone module. Without the empty element Maven first looks
         for ../pom.xml, fails to find it, and the parent-resolution failure then surfaces
         confusingly as a cascade of "managed version is missing" errors. The empty element tells
         Maven to skip the filesystem search and resolve the parent from the repository. -->
    <!-- 3.5.16 is the last published OSS 3.5.x release and is a deliberate pin, not staleness.
         Two of the versions it manages are overridden in <properties> below under a documented
         security exception; that exception overrides BOM PROPERTIES and does not change this
         parent, its version, or the rejection of the 4.x line. -->
    <parent>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
        <version>3.5.16</version>
        <relativePath/>
    </parent>

    <!-- groupId and artifactId derive from the mandated root package com.vsergeychik.carddemo.
         Version 1.0.0 aligns with the source version footer "Ver: CardDemo_v1.0-15-g27d6c6f-68"
         carried by app/cbl/CSUTLDTC.cbl, app/jcl/INTCALC.jcl and app/jcl/POSTTRAN.jcl. It is a
         fixed release identifier by design (practice B1). -->
    <groupId>com.vsergeychik</groupId>
    <artifactId>carddemo</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0</version>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <name>carddemo</name>
    <description>
        AWS CardDemo - like-for-like migration of 28 COBOL programs to Java 21 and Spring Boot
        3.x: 17 CICS online transactions as stateless REST controllers, 9 non-CICS programs as
        Spring Batch jobs (8 invoked by EXEC PGM= plus the untriggered CBTRN01C orphan), 2 CALLed
        subprograms as injected components, and 12 dataset repositories over plain JDBC with no
        schema change.
    </description>

    <properties>
        <!-- Java 21 (LTS). The parent defaults java.version to 17 and derives
             maven.compiler.release from it; both are overridden explicitly so the target release
             is unambiguous however the build is invoked (practice B8). -->
        <java.version>21</java.version>
        <maven.compiler.release>21</maven.compiler.release>

        <!-- Source-file encoding, stated explicitly so the build never depends on the platform
             default. This is the encoding of the *Java sources* only. It is emphatically NOT the
             mainframe *data* charset: the EBCDIC and ASCII dataset charsets are named explicitly
             at runtime by the configuration and the codec classes under src, and must never be
             conflated with, or configured through, this property (practice B8). -->
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
        <project.reporting.outputEncoding>UTF-8</project.reporting.outputEncoding>

        <!-- The three explicitly-versioned build plugins (AAP 0.5.4). Each version appears
             exactly once, here, so it can be audited in one place. Note that the parent BOM
             manages maven-compiler-plugin but NOT maven-surefire-plugin, and defines no jacoco
             property at all, so all three pins are load-bearing. -->
        <maven-compiler-plugin.version>3.15.0</maven-compiler-plugin.version>
        <maven-surefire-plugin.version>3.5.6</maven-surefire-plugin.version>
        <jacoco.version>0.8.15</jacoco.version>

        <!-- The parity project's primary quality bar: at least 90% BRANCH coverage. Declared once
             so it is auditable, and applied below at BOTH bundle and package granularity.
             DO NOT LOWER THIS VALUE - it is the mandated acceptance gate (G49). -->
        <jacoco.branch.minimum>0.90</jacoco.branch.minimum>

        <!-- Directory-safe form of the build timestamp, which surefire below passes to the test
             JVM as carddemo.test.run-id so each run owns its own output directory. Maven's default
             format is an ISO instant containing colons, which is not a portable path segment; this
             keeps the value sortable, readable and usable as a directory name. It is the only
             consumer of this property, and no observable test output reads it. -->
        <maven.build.timestamp.format>yyyyMMdd-HHmmssSSS</maven.build.timestamp.format>

        <!-- ============================================================================
             NO PARENT BOM VERSION PROPERTY IS REDECLARED HERE, AND NONE MAY BE.

             spring-boot-dependencies-3.5.16.pom declares tomcat.version as 10.1.55 and
             jackson-bom.version as 2.21.4, and AAP 0.5.4 pins the resolved inventory at
             exactly those values. Redeclaring either property here is version MANAGEMENT:
             it moves the whole family off the frozen inventory just as surely as naming a
             <version> on a dependency would, and it does so without appearing in the
             dependency block where a reader would look for it.

             The advisory position on both families, and why it is not closed here, is
             recorded in this file's header under "THE DEPENDENCY INVENTORY IS FROZEN". It
             is a plan-level decision, not a property this file may add.
             ============================================================================ -->

        <!--
          THE TEST RUN IDENTITY, and the reason a second invocation cannot inherit the first's
          directory.

          Every dataset a test run WRITES is rooted at carddemo.test.work-dir in
          src/main/resources/application-test.yml, and that root ends in this value: WHICH
          invocation produced it. It is derived from the build timestamp, so a second
          "mvn verify" on the same clone starts from a fresh directory whose name no other process
          could have guessed in advance, instead of inheriting whatever the previous run left
          behind. The other axis - WHICH checkout - is supplied by application-test.yml from the
          CLONE_INDEX environment variable, because only Spring's placeholder syntax can carry a
          default for an environment variable that may legitimately be unset.

          Unpredictability is the security property here, not tidiness: a fixed
          ${java.io.tmpdir}/carddemo/test-datasets root in a world-writable sticky /tmp can be
          pre-created as a symlink by any other user on the host, which redirects every write the
          suite performs (CWE-59), and can be read or overwritten by a concurrent run (CWE-377,
          CWE-362). config/TestWorkspaceIsolationTest enforces the rest of the contract - the
          directory is created owner-only, opened without following links, and cleaned up only
          within the directory this run owns.
        -->
        <carddemo.test.run.id>${maven.build.timestamp}</carddemo.test.run.id>

        <!--
          THE ZERO-TEST FLOOR (gate G54, practice B7).

          surefire's own failIfNoTests is set below; this is the second half of the same guard.
          It is the number of *test classes* the suite is known to contain, and the sentinel test
          asserts the discovered count is at least this. It is a FLOOR, never an equality: adding a
          suite must never require editing the pom, but silently losing three quarters of the
          suite to a mis-scoped <includes> or a broken discovery configuration must fail the build
          rather than report a green run over whatever survived.
        -->
        <carddemo.test.suite-floor>60</carddemo.test.suite-floor>
    </properties>

    <!--
      Exactly six dependencies, no more. Not one of them names a <version>: every version is
      managed by the spring-boot-starter-parent 3.5.16 BOM (practice B1).
    -->
    <dependencies>
        <!-- Online layer. The 17 CICS pseudo-conversational programs become stateless REST
             controllers whose request and response fields are a 1:1 projection of the 441 BMS
             field definitions. Conversation state travels in the payload, never in a server-side
             session, so a servlet web stack is all that is required. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Batch layer. The 8 programs invoked by EXEC PGM= in app/jcl and app/proc, plus the
             untriggered CBTRN01C, become NINE Spring Batch jobs whose step boundaries,
             DD-to-resource bindings, PARM parsing and COND gating mirror the JCL. CBCUS01C is one
             of those eight - READCUST.jcl invokes it - and is not an additional job; the header's
             job-count section records the AAP-prose discrepancy this resolves.
             This starter supplies Spring Batch 5.2.6 transitively; spring-batch-core is
             deliberately NOT declared here and must never be pinned to the 6.x line, which
             belongs to the Spring Boot 4 BOM (practice B2). -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-batch</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Data access. JdbcTemplate and a pooled DataSource for the 12 dataset repositories.
             Plain JDBC by mandate: the record layouts stay exactly as the 28 copybooks define
             them, so no mapping framework is introduced and no schema is ever created. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-jdbc</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Bean Validation for the 17 request payloads. Field lengths come from the symbolic-map
             "xxxI PIC X(n)" items, so validation is the enforcement point for screen field
             widths that CICS used to enforce for free. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Test aggregate: JUnit 5 Jupiter, Mockito, AssertJ and the Spring test support. Tests
             are a first-class deliverable authored alongside the code, not bolted on afterwards
             (practice B10): this drives the 28 parameterised parity test classes, their 560
             declarative cases and every per-package unit test. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!-- TEST SCOPE ONLY, and it must stay that way. Two jobs, both confined to the test
             lifecycle: it backs the Spring Batch JobRepository (Batch 5 requires a
             DataSource-backed repository, so a batch test context will not start without one),
             and it hosts the parity harness's seeded fixture rows. It is never promoted to
             runtime or compile scope, because production reaches the existing datasets through
             the deployment-supplied driver described in the header (AAP 0.5.5, risk R-E). -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.h2database</groupId>
            <artifactId>h2</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <!-- Stable artifact name, so the documented run command does not have to be rewritten
             whenever the module version changes. -->
        <finalName>carddemo</finalName>

        <!--
          TEST RESOURCES: the module's own, plus the COBOL copybooks that serve as read-only
          transcription oracles.

          Declaring this element replaces Maven's default, so src/test/resources is restated
          first and unfiltered - the nine fixed-width fixtures and the parity case files are
          byte-exact parity data, and resource filtering would corrupt them the moment one of them
          contained a ${...} sequence.

          The second entry puts app/cpy/CSLKPCDY.cpy on the TEST CLASSPATH under cobol-oracle/.
          AreaCodeLookupTest proves the 1,318-line area-code table was transcribed correctly by
          reading the copybook itself rather than a copy of it, and before this entry existed it
          located the file by walking up from the process working directory. That made a correct
          transcription fail whenever the working directory was not the module directory - a
          different checkout layout, a nested clone, an IDE launched from the repository root - so
          the suite was reporting on the runner's filesystem rather than on the code. Reaching the
          oracle through the classpath makes the lookup positional-independent and immutable: the
          copybook is COPIED into target/test-classes at build time and app/cpy is never written
          to, which keeps practice B3 intact.

          Only the one member is included. Widening this to app/cpy/*.cpy would put 28 copybooks
          on the test classpath for no reason and invite a test to start reading a copybook it has
          no oracle relationship with.
        -->
        <testResources>
            <testResource>
                <directory>${project.basedir}/src/test/resources</directory>
                <filtering>false</filtering>
            </testResource>
            <testResource>
                <directory>${project.basedir}/../cpy</directory>
                <targetPath>cobol-oracle</targetPath>
                <filtering>false</filtering>
                <includes>
                    <include>CSLKPCDY.cpy</include>
                </includes>
            </testResource>
        </testResources>

        <!-- Plugin ORDER is significant: surefire is declared before jacoco so that, within the
             shared test phase, the tests run first and the coverage report is generated from the
             execution data they produced. -->
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${maven-compiler-plugin.version}</version>
                <configuration>
                    <!-- <release> rather than the <source>/<target> pair: release additionally
                         validates that only Java 21 platform APIs are referenced, which is what
                         "targets Java 21 (LTS)" actually has to mean. No preview features are
                         enabled and no annotation processor is configured, because there is no
                         compile-time code generator in the closed dependency set. -->
                    <release>21</release>
                    <!-- Retain parameter names in the bytecode so payload binding and diagnostics
                         report the real field names rather than arg0, arg1 (practice B8). -->
                    <parameters>true</parameters>
                    <compilerArgs>
                        <!-- Advisory only. Warnings are surfaced, never promoted to errors: a
                             like-for-like translation legitimately produces constructs the
                             compiler would rather see written differently, and failing the build
                             on them would pressure implementers into changing behaviour. -->
                        <arg>-Xlint:all</arg>
                    </compilerArgs>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>

            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${maven-surefire-plugin.version}</version>
                <configuration>
                    <!-- Results stream to stdout instead of report files, so a single
                         non-interactive command shows every failure inline (practice B7, gate
                         G54). There is no watch mode and nothing here waits on terminal input. -->
                    <useFile>false</useFile>
                    <!-- Full stack traces: when a parity case fails, the diff has to be traceable
                         to the exact translation decision that caused it. -->
                    <trimStackTrace>false</trimStackTrace>
                    <!-- Deterministic, filesystem-independent execution order, so a parity run is
                         reproducible across machines. -->
                    <runOrder>alphabetical</runOrder>
                    <!-- ONE OWNED OUTPUT DIRECTORY PER RUN. application-test.yml derives
                         carddemo.test.work-dir from carddemo.test.run-id, and several outputs
                         beneath it are written in append mode - the job-submission port is
                         DISPOSITION(MOD) by CSD contract. Without a per-run discriminator a fresh
                         run would append to, and read back, the previous run's files, which makes
                         a parity diff wrong without raising any error.

                         A system property is used because it outranks the profile document in
                         Spring Boot's property source order, so the profile can carry an honest
                         literal default for a run started outside Maven. The timestamp is stable
                         for the whole invocation and shared by every fork, so one run means one
                         directory; ${random.uuid} would not do, because it yields a new value on
                         every placeholder resolution and work-dir is referenced about thirty
                         times. The clone dimension is handled separately, in the profile, from
                         the CLONE_INDEX environment variable. -->
                    <!-- The default includes already match both the per-package *Test classes and
                         the 28 *ParityTest classes, so <includes> is intentionally left alone.
                         Narrowing it would silently drop parity coverage. -->

                    <!--
                      A BUILD THAT DISCOVERED NO TEST MUST FAIL.

                      The default is false, and with it "mvn clean verify" reports success on a
                      run in which nothing was executed: surefire is content to find no test, and
                      jacoco:check skips its rules outright when no execution data was produced,
                      so both halves of the gate stand down together and the build goes green over
                      zero evidence. That is the single worst failure mode a parity gate can have,
                      because the gate is stated as "diff count = 0" and a suite that never ran
                      reports exactly that.

                      True here, permanently. It is paired with the sentinel test, which asserts
                      the discovered suite is not merely non-empty but complete:
                      failIfNoTests catches "no tests at all", the sentinel catches "far fewer
                      tests than this module contains".
                    -->
                    <failIfNoTests>true</failIfNoTests>

                    <systemPropertyVariables>
                        <!-- The per-invocation identity read by application-test.yml when it
                             composes carddemo.test.work-dir. A system property is used because it
                             outranks the profile document in Spring Boot's property source order,
                             so the profile can carry an honest literal default for a run started
                             outside Maven. The timestamp is stable for the whole invocation and
                             shared by every fork, so one run means one directory; ${random.uuid}
                             would not do, because it yields a new value on every placeholder
                             resolution and work-dir is referenced about thirty times. The clone
                             dimension is handled separately, in the profile, from the CLONE_INDEX
                             environment variable. -->
                        <carddemo.test.run-id>${maven.build.timestamp}</carddemo.test.run-id>
                        <!-- The per-invocation identity behind carddemo.test.work-dir. Passed as a
                             system property rather than baked into a resource file so that the
                             value belongs to the RUN and not to the checkout: two invocations of
                             the same clone get different writable roots. See the property
                             declaration above for the CWE-59 / CWE-377 / CWE-362 reasoning. -->
                        <carddemo.test.run.id>${carddemo.test.run.id}</carddemo.test.run.id>
                        <!-- The floor the sentinel test asserts, supplied from the single
                             declaration above so the number lives in exactly one place. -->
                        <carddemo.test.suite-floor>${carddemo.test.suite-floor}</carddemo.test.suite-floor>
                    </systemPropertyVariables>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>

            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
                <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${jacoco.version}</version>
                <executions>
                    <!-- Instrument before the tests run. prepare-agent binds to initialize by
                         default and sets the argLine that surefire then picks up. -->
                    <execution>
                        <id>jacoco-prepare-agent</id>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>prepare-agent</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>

                    <!-- Human-readable and machine-readable reports, produced in the test phase
                         immediately after surefire. -->
                    <execution>
                        <id>jacoco-report</id>
                        <phase>test</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>report</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>

                    <!--
                      THE COVERAGE GATE. Bound to verify because "mvn clean verify" is the single
                      documented gating command (practice B7) - not to test, and not to install.

                      Two rules, deliberately, and both are required:
                        * BUNDLE  - the module as a whole must clear the bar.
                        * PACKAGE - EVERY package must clear it independently. In a single-module
                                    build a BUNDLE-only rule would let a well-covered package mask
                                    an untested one, which is exactly the outcome gate G49
                                    forbids when it says no module may hide behind another's
                                    coverage. The package rule is what actually delivers that
                                    guarantee here, across config, common, account, customer,
                                    transaction, billing, card, user, admin, statement and util.

                      The counter is BRANCH, not LINE and not INSTRUCTION: the parity surface is
                      508 88-level condition names, 111 EVALUATE statements and the guard chains
                      around them, and only branch coverage evidences that both sides of each of
                      those decisions were actually exercised.
                    -->
                    <execution>
                        <id>jacoco-check-branch-coverage</id>
                        <phase>verify</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>check</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <!-- Stated explicitly: a gate that cannot fail the build is not a
                                 gate (practice B8). -->
                            <haltOnFailure>true</haltOnFailure>
                            <rules>
                                <rule>
                                    <element>BUNDLE</element>
                                    <limits>
                                        <limit>
                                            <counter>BRANCH</counter>
                                            <value>COVEREDRATIO</value>
                                            <minimum>${jacoco.branch.minimum}</minimum>
                                        </limit>
                                    </limits>
                                </rule>
                                <rule>
                                    <element>PACKAGE</element>
                                    <limits>
                                        <limit>
                                            <counter>BRANCH</counter>
                                            <value>COVEREDRATIO</value>
                                            <minimum>${jacoco.branch.minimum}</minimum>
                                        </limit>
                                    </limits>
                                </rule>
                            </rules>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>

            <!-- Declared WITHOUT a <version>: both the version and the repackage execution come
                 from the parent's pluginManagement, so this adds no new coordinate and no fourth
                 explicit plugin version. It produces the runnable Boot artifact and backs
                 spring-boot:run. mainClass is pinned explicitly rather than left to main-class
                 scanning, which removes any ambiguity about the entry point (practice B8). -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
                <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <configuration>
                    <mainClass>com.vsergeychik.carddemo.CardDemoApplication</mainClass>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
